A||||3||GT Bastard||Air bubbles in clutch system.... ||||4||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||06-27-2000||08:09 PM||||A friend of mine has an '86 SE V6 w/3.1 and a tranny from a Cavalier that's been getting air bubbles in the clutch system daily.  He's apparently replaced clutch slave and master cylinder very recently!  If you bleed the system, it's fine for about a 1/2 day or so...  Any ideas? Anyone had problems with the clutch line itself?<P>Thanks in advance ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Sillhouette.com||06-27-2000||10:19 PM||||I don't have any bubbles in the clutch fluid however if I drive a long time at highway speeds the clutch becomes very soft.  It only returns to normal when I pump it a lot and shift.  The explanation I got was that the slave by the transmission was mounted very close to the Ypipe for the exhaust and the fluid in it starts boiling.  So until you replace that fluid with cooler on from the reservoir you get that spungy feeling.  This might also be your bubble problem.  Change the fluid to some high performance red line stuff that can take extreme temperature hopefully that helps.  I did not have time to change mine yet so I'm not sure if that is a fix.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000002||coyote||06-28-2000||01:17 PM||||GT:<BR>There is no signaficant differences between the clutch line and brake lines, it is possible that there is a soft spot on the line. If memory seerves it does run under the rear jack point of the car. that would be the first place i would inspect. the other thing to check for is cross threading of the fittings. A stock line also has a soft hose in-between the hard lines, that part of it is around the steering column. Hard to inspect but maybe worthwhile ti check out.  Good luck and let us know what you find<P>Coyote||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||06-29-2000||01:08 PM||||I'll bring you a quart of the AGIP DOT 5.1 i use in my/your car... thats good to over 1500* after the install of that i never had a Fluid related clutch problem again. BUt like Coyote said.. make sure the threads aren't crossed and the "soft" lines aren't crappy and weaping/split/craking etc.||||reg||1
